.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt SCHED_SETSCHEDULER 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sched_setscheduler ,
.Nm sched_getscheduler ,
.Nm sched_setparam ,
.Nm sched_getparam ,
.Nm sched_get_priority_max ,
.Nm sched_get_priority_min ,
.Nm sched_rr_get_interval ,
.Nm sched_setaffinity ,
.Nm sched_getaffinity ,
.Nm sched_yield ,
.Nm getcpu
.Nd scheduling policy, priority, affinity and yielding
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sched.h
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setscheduler "pid_t pid" "int policy" "const struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getscheduler "pid_t pid"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setparam "pid_t pid" "const struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getparam "pid_t pid" "struct sched_param *param"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_get_priority_max "int policy"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_get_priority_min "int policy"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval "pid_t pid" "struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_setaffinity "pid_t pid" "size_t cpusetsize" "const cpu_set_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_getaffinity "pid_t pid" "size_t cpusetsize" "cpu_set_t *mask"
.Ft int
.Fn sched_yield "void"
.Ft int
.Fn getcpu "unsigned int *cpu" "unsigned int *node"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The scheduler gives every ready process the processor in turn, a tick at
a time, and has one policy,
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
with priority 0.
The machine has one processor, CPU 0.
A
.Fa pid
of 0 names the caller; any other names a process that has not been
reaped.
.Pp
.Fn sched_getscheduler
returns
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
.Pq 0
for every process, and
.Fn sched_getparam
stores its priority, 0.
.Fn sched_setscheduler
and
.Fn sched_setparam
accept
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
with priority 0, the setting every process already has, from a caller
that may change the process: one with effective user ID 0, or whose
effective user ID equals the process's real or effective user ID.
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
may be added to the policy and is not kept, since a fork has no other
policy to reset.
The other policies are refused:
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR
with a priority from 1 to 99 with
.Er EPERM ,
and every other policy and priority with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
and
.Fn sched_get_priority_min
give Linux's range for each policy Linux has, whether or not it runs here:
1 to 99 for
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR ,
and 0 to 0 for
.Dv SCHED_OTHER ,
.Dv SCHED_BATCH ,
.Dv SCHED_IDLE ,
.Dv SCHED_DEADLINE
and
.Dv SCHED_EXT .
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval
stores the time slice, one 10 ms tick, since the timer takes the
processor from the running process at every tick.
.Pp
.Fn sched_getaffinity
stores a mask with CPU 0 alone.
.Fn sched_setaffinity
accepts a mask that has CPU 0, whatever it says of CPUs the machine does
not have, from a caller that may change the process, and changes nothing,
since every process runs on CPU 0.
.Pp
.Fn getcpu ,
number 318, stores 0 for the CPU and 0 for the NUMA node where its
non-null arguments point.
.Pp
.Fn sched_yield
puts the caller behind the other ready processes in the round-robin
order.
It returns when the caller's turn comes again, at once when no other
process is ready.
.Ss Differences from Linux
Linux grants
.Dv SCHED_BATCH
and
.Dv SCHED_IDLE
to any process that may change the target, and
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
and
.Dv SCHED_RR
to a caller with
.Dv CAP_SYS_NICE
or a
.Dv RLIMIT_RTPRIO
that allows the priority.
This kernel has one policy and refuses them all, to root too.
Linux reports
.Dv SCHED_RESET_ON_FORK
back once it has been set.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 154 for
.Fn sched_setparam ,
155 for
.Fn sched_getparam ,
156 for
.Fn sched_setscheduler ,
157 for
.Fn sched_getscheduler ,
158 for
.Fn sched_yield ,
159 for
.Fn sched_get_priority_max ,
160 for
.Fn sched_get_priority_min ,
161 for
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval ,
241 for
.Fn sched_setaffinity ,
and 242 for
.Fn sched_getaffinity .
The raw
.Fn sched_getaffinity
returns the bytes of the mask it wrote, four, and fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a
.Fa cpusetsize
that is zero or not a multiple of four; the C library clears the rest of
a larger set and returns 0.
The raw
.Fn sched_setaffinity
reads at most four bytes of the mask.
.Fn sched_rr_get_interval
writes a 32-bit timespec.
.Pp
musl implements
.Fn sched_setscheduler ,
.Fn sched_getscheduler ,
.Fn sched_setparam
and
.Fn sched_getparam
as failures with
.Er ENOSYS
and does not call the kernel, since Linux's calls act on a thread and
POSIX's on a process; glibc calls it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sched_getscheduler
returns the policy,
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
and
.Fn sched_get_priority_min
the priority, and the other functions 0.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/schedmusl.c
yields, reads the policy, the parameter, the priority ranges, the time
slice and the affinity, sets
.Dv SCHED_OTHER
and an affinity with CPU 0, and checks the refusals of the other policies,
of an affinity without CPU 0, and of another user's process.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A parameter, time slice or mask cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A
.Fa pid
is negative, a
.Fa param
is null, a policy or priority is one this kernel does not run, a policy
number is unknown to
.Fn sched_get_priority_max
or
.Fn sched_get_priority_min ,
a
.Fa cpusetsize
for
.Fn sched_getaffinity
is zero or not a multiple of four, or a mask for
.Fn sched_setaffinity
lacks CPU 0.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller may not change the process, or asked for
.Dv SCHED_FIFO
or
.Dv SCHED_RR .
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No process has the ID
.Fa pid .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getpriority 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
